# AeroDash Traceability Enforcement

You are enforcing the **Single Traceability Contract (STC)** defined in
`docs/stc.md`. That document is the sole authority for ontology, graph
rules, tag formats, and lifecycle rules.

**Do NOT invent rules not present in the STC. Do NOT contradict the STC.**

---

## 1. Implementation (IMP) Rules

When creating or modifying a source file in `frontend/src/**/*.ts`
(excluding `*.spec.ts` and `*.int.spec.ts`):

- **Tag format:** `// @IMP-{MODULE}-{LAYER}-{NUMBER}@ (FROM: @REQ-xxx@)` or
  `// @IMP-{MODULE}-{LAYER}-{NUMBER}@ (FROM: @DES-xxx@)` — both REQ and DES
  may appear in a single `FROM` clause.
- **Upstream reference:** The `FROM` clause MUST reference at least one REQ or
  DES. An IMP without any upstream trace is forbidden (STC INV-001).
- **Placement (STC §3.4):** Immediately before the traced code block
  (export, function, statement, or branch). May appear at any indentation
  level within the file.
- **ID assignment:** Use the next sequential number in the
  `{MODULE}-{LAYER}` namespace. Check the registry at
  `trace/implementation/{module}.yaml` to determine the next available ID.

---

## 2. Unit Test (UT) Rules

When creating or modifying a test file matching `*.spec.ts`
(excluding `*.int.spec.ts`):

- **Tag format:**
  `// @UT-{MODULE}-{LAYER}-{NUMBER}@ (FROM: @IMP-xxx@[, @IMP-xxx@])`
- **Upstream reference:** MUST trace to one or more IMP tags.
  MUST NOT trace directly to REQ, DES, UJ, or H (STC §2.2).
- **Placement (STC §3.4):** Immediately before the `it` / `it.each` call
  or the scenario entry in a data-driven array. One tag per test case,
  not per `describe` block.
- **Isolation (STC Appendix B.1):** Unit tests MUST execute in absolute
  isolation — no network, no disk I/O, no browser APIs, no real Pinia stores.
- **Scope:** Each `describe` block SHOULD verify one logical unit of one or
  more IMP artifacts.

---

## 3. Integration Test (IT) Rules

When creating or modifying a test file matching `*.int.spec.ts`:

- **Tag format:**
  `// @IT-{MODULE}-{LAYER}-{NUMBER}@ (FROM: @IMP-xxx@[, @IMP-xxx@])`
- **Upstream reference:** MUST trace to one or more IMP tags.
  MUST NOT trace directly to REQ, DES, UJ, or H (STC §2.2).
- **Placement (STC §3.4):** Immediately before the `it` / `it.each` call
  or the scenario entry in a data-driven array. One tag per test case,
  not per `describe` block.
- **Interaction scope (STC Appendix B.2):** Integration tests MUST verify the
  handshake between two or more internal modules. External network calls MUST
  be mocked.

---

## 4. E2E Test (E2E) Rules

When creating or modifying a `.feature` file in `frontend/tests/e2e/`:

### 4.1 Trace Comment (Required)

Every `Scenario` MUST have a trace comment as a Gherkin `#` comment on the
line immediately above the execution tags:

**Business E2E:**

```gherkin
# @E2E-{PHASE}-{NUMBER}@ (FROM: @UJ-xxx@)
```

**Technical E2E:**

```gherkin
# @E2E-{PHASE or Module}-{NUMBER}@ (TECHNICAL)
```

### 4.2 Execution Tags (Required)

Execution tags are placed on the line between the trace comment and the
`Scenario` keyword:

| Tag | Purpose | Required for |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `@UJ-xxx` | Source User Journey | Business E2E only |
| `@phase-X` | Development phase | All E2E |
| `@e2e` | Test type marker | Business E2E |
| `@smoke` | Smoke test marker | Technical E2E (smoke) |
| `@technical-e2e` | Technical test marker | Technical E2E (non-smoke) |
| `@module-xxx` | Module scope | Recommended |
| `@happy-path` / `@edge-case` | Scenario classification | Recommended |

### 4.3 Business vs. Technical E2E

- **Business E2E** validates user-facing behavior. MUST trace to UJ.
  MUST follow the full MTM chain: `H → REQ → UJ → E2E`.
- **Technical E2E** validates system health (boot, connectivity, smoke).
  MUST NOT be used for business validation. MUST be tagged `@smoke` or
  `@technical-e2e`.

### 4.4 E2E Trace Placement

- Trace tags MUST be placed in the `.feature` file ONLY.
- Trace tags MUST NEVER be placed in step definition files (`*.ts`).
- The UJ execution tag (e.g., `@UJ-B-005`) MUST match the UJ cited in the
  trace comment `FROM` clause.

---

## 5. Registry Enforcement

When creating a **new** traced artifact (IMP, UT, IT, or E2E), you MUST update
the corresponding YAML registry in the `trace/` directory as part of the same
operation.

| Artifact | Registry Location | Required Fields |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| IMP | `trace/implementation/{module}.yaml` | ID, title, req and/or des, files |
| UT | `trace/unit_test/{module}.yaml` | ID, title, impl, files |
| IT | `trace/integration_test/{module}.yaml` | ID, title, impl, files |
| E2E | `trace/e2e/{phase-or-domain}.yaml` | ID, title, files |

**Registry rules:**

- **ID assignment:** Use the next sequential number in the namespace. Inspect
  the registry file to determine the next available ID.
- **Deletion:** Mark deleted entries as `deleted` or `obsolete`. Never remove
  lines.
- **No ID reuse:** Once assigned, an ID is permanent even if the artifact is
  deleted.
- **Ranges:** Bulk deletions may use range notation:
  `{ID-START} - {ID-END}` followed by `deleted`.

---

## 6. Forbidden Patterns

### 6.1 Missing Tags

- NEVER create an implementation file without an `@IMP-xxx@` trace tag.
- NEVER create a test file without the corresponding `@UT-xxx@`, `@IT-xxx@`,
  or `@E2E-xxx@` tag.
- NEVER create a `.feature` Scenario without both the trace comment AND
  execution tags.

### 6.2 Wrong Trace Direction (STC §2.2)

- NEVER trace UT / IT to REQ or DES (must go through IMP).
- NEVER trace E2E to IMP (must go through UJ).
- NEVER trace IMP to H (must go through REQ or DES).
- NEVER trace UT to IT or E2E.
- NEVER trace IT to UT or E2E.

### 6.3 Cross-Layer Contamination (STC §3.4)

- NEVER place E2E trace tags in step definition `.ts` files.
- NEVER place IMP tags in test files (`*.spec.ts`, `*.int.spec.ts`).
- NEVER place UT / IT tags in non-test implementation files.

### 6.4 Orphan Artifacts

- NEVER create an IMP without at least one upstream REQ or DES.
- NEVER create a UT / IT without at least one upstream IMP.
- NEVER create a business E2E without at least one upstream UJ.

### 6.5 ID Violations

- NEVER reuse a deleted or obsolete ID.
- NEVER create duplicate IDs within the same node type.
- NEVER skip registry updates when creating new artifacts.

---

## 7. STC Invariant Coverage

The following STC invariants are enforced by this rule at the agent level:

| STC Invariant | Enforcement | Cursor Section |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| INV-001 (no orphan IMP) | Agent-enforced | §1, §6.4 |
| INV-002 (no orphan UT) | Agent-enforced | §2, §6.4 |
| INV-003 (no orphan IT) | Agent-enforced | §3, §6.4 |
| INV-004 (no orphan business E2E) | Agent-enforced | §4, §6.4 |
| INV-005 (no unverified IMP) | CI-only | *Requires full graph analysis* |
| INV-006 (no UJ without REQ) | CI-only | *UJ/REQ files outside agent glob scope* |
| INV-007 (unique IDs per prefix namespace) | Agent-enforced | §5, §6.5 |
| INV-008 (single-source definition) | Agent-enforced | §5, §6.5 |
| INV-009 (no dangling FROM references) | CI-only | *Requires full graph analysis* |
| INV-010 (no circular dependencies) | CI-only | *Requires full graph analysis* |
| INV-011 (edge type conformance) | Agent-enforced | §6.2 |

Invariants marked "CI-only" require whole-repository graph analysis and cannot
be validated by the agent during single-file operations. They are enforced by
the CI pipeline (STC §7.3).

